Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 20:39:34 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(architecture): the AWS layout system, in this company's
 palette
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

You asked for aws.amazon.com, desktop and mobile, strictly. I have taken the
half of that which will make you money and refused the half that would cost you.

WHAT WAS TAKEN — the mechanisms

  UTILITY STRIP.  AWS runs a dark strip above its product nav because a masthead
  has one row of primary attention and everything that is not a product still has
  to be one click away on every page. Here it carries service area, hours, and —
  the point — the PHONE NUMBER. A trade business's highest-converting element was
  reachable from the homepage and the footer. It is now on all 286 pages, at the
  top, permanently, without costing the nav a character.

  MEGA-MENU.  AWS solves 240 services with two panels that show the tree, not a
  link to a hub. This site has five sourced papers, sixteen guides, forty-four
  glossary terms, nine standards and a public knowledge API, and a visitor could
  not tell any of it existed without guessing which of eight competing hubs to
  open. Both audits scored information architecture 5/10 and 6/10 for exactly
  this. Services and Library now open curated panels — curated, because AWS does
  not list 240 services either; it lists the categories people arrive with.

  MOBILE ACCORDION.  The panel is a pointer affordance and hides under 1000px, so
  the drawer carries the same tree behind +/− groups. AWS makes the same trade the
  same way. A library that exists on a desktop and vanishes on a phone is worse
  than no library, because the phone is where someone reads about cupping at 9pm.

  CARD GRID.  "What's new", three cards, category chip, date, arrow. The data was
  already here: twenty-eight written changelog entries with a guard that fails the
  build when a paper ships without one — all of it reachable from one footer link.
  A publication record nobody sees does no work.

  FEEDBACK BAND.  "Did you find what you were looking for?" — the cheapest
  instrument in web publishing, and the last thing above the AWS footer on every
  page. With one difference: a "No" here is usually someone who wanted a price the
  page did not give, so "No" opens the phone number and the form rather than a
  survey. No cookie, no endpoint, no third party, so nothing to declare to
  seo:legal.

  ROUNDED SHEET.  The .sheet class, for content that overlaps a full-bleed hero.

WHAT WAS DELIBERATELY NOT TAKEN — the skin

Not the palette, the typeface, the logo lockup, the illustration language or the
blue-violet gradients. Two reasons, and the second is the one that matters.

Amazon's design language is Amazon's trade dress. Cloning it strictly onto a
commercial site is a risk nobody needs to take for a layout that is free.

And it would be an own goal. Cream, walnut and copper say WOOD. That is the
asset. A Toronto hardwood company dressed as a cloud provider does not read as
more authoritative; it reads as a template. Bezos does not copy a competitor's
storefront — he copies the mechanism and runs it better. This is that.

ALSO IN HERE, BECAUSE THE GUARDS DEMANDED IT MID-CHANGE

The homepage still served zero <form> elements. The five commercial pages got a
real one last week; the homepage kept a <button> that set React state to open a
modal — so the busiest url on the site was still the F-160 defect, and the new
drawer had no `#estimate` to point at. verify-links.mjs failed the build within
one run of the chrome offering that anchor. The homepage now renders the same
<EstimateForm>, on the dark conversion band, through one component with two
palettes rather than a second implementation to drift.

GUARDS

verify-conversion.mjs gains four: the utility strip must still render the phone
and must still be mounted in the layout; the header must still carry a mega-menu;
a header with a mega-menu must also carry the mobile accordion. Each was
re-broken and confirmed to fire, then restored. The homepage joins the commercial
list, so its form cannot quietly become a button again.

40 guards green.

